@database "tool_preload"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: tool_preload.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Tool Preload"
@{h1}Tool Preload

Some agents always open a turn the same way. Asked "change the intro in
document plan-a", the first thing the model does is load plan-a -- a full
LLM round trip, paid for with the prompt tokens of the whole context, whose
outcome was known before it started. Tool Preload makes those opening calls
itself: a rule in the agent's YAML matches the user's message with a regular
expression, the tools it names run before the first LLM call, and their
results go into the conversation exactly as if the model had called them.
The model's first answer then starts where its second would have.

The plugin has no tools and no panel. It is installed and on in the shipped
configuration, but does nothing for an agent until that agent's YAML
switches it on and gives it rules.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Writing rules  " link rules}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}, as shipped:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    tool_preload:
      type: tool_preload
      enabled: true
@{body}

No settings go here: the plugin reads none from its entry. No allowlist line
for the plugin itself -- it has no tools. No extra packages, no data of its
own.

@{h2}Switching it on for an agent

Everything happens in the agent's YAML, under the full hook name
@{tt}tool_preload.preload@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "notes/*"
  hooks:
    overrides:
      tool_preload.preload:
        enabled: true
        rules:
          - match: "document\\s+(?P<doc>[\\w-]+)"
            tool: notes_manage_json
            params:
              operation: read
              doc: "{doc}"
@{body}

The tools a rule names must be in the agent's own allowlist -- a preload may
call exactly what the model may call (see @{" The hook " link hook}).

@{h2}Settings

All of them come from the agent's override. They are read on every call, so
an edited agent YAML takes effect as soon as the agent's configuration is
reloaded.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
rules | none | the rules, tried in order; without rules the hook does nothing. See @{" Writing rules " link rules}
max_calls_per_turn | 5 | the most tool calls all rules together make in one turn
dedup | true | skip a rule whose calls are all already in the conversation
max_seconds | 45 | seconds the preload of a turn may take, counted from the start of the hook; once used up, no further call starts. Anything but a positive number means 45
@{body}

@{tt}enabled@{utt} switches the hook on; @{tt}timeout@{utt} and
@{tt}order@{utt} in the agent's override are ignored. A plugin-wide key
(@{tt}tool_preload:@{utt} instead of @{tt}tool_preload.preload:@{utt}) does
nothing here.

@{h2}Trying it

@{tt}config/agents/tool_preload_test_agent.yaml@{utt} is a complete test
agent with a store and one rule, shipped switched off; the file says how to
run it. The proof that it works: the agent names the document's content,
and the only read in its session is a call whose id starts with
@{tt}preload_@{utt}.
@endnode

@node rules "Writing rules"
@{h1}Writing rules

A rule is a regular expression and the calls to make when it matches:

@{code yaml}
rules:
  # one call: the shorthand
  - match: "document\\s+(?P<doc>[\\w-]+)"
    tool: notes_manage_json
    params: {operation: read, doc: "{doc}"}

  # several calls: run one after the other, in this order
  - match: "chapter\\s+(?P<n>\\d+)"
    calls:
      - tool: project_set_var
        params: {name: current_chapter, value: "{n}"}
      - tool: project_get_chapter
        params: {chapter: "{n}"}
@{body}

@{h2}Matching

@{bullet}@{tt}match@{utt} is a Python regular expression, searched anywhere
in the message (@{tt}re.search@{utt}), case-sensitive unless the pattern
says @{tt}(?i)@{utt}.
@{bullet}It sees the text of the user's message only -- the text parts of a
message with images -- and only its first 2000 characters. A trigger further
down is not seen.
@{bullet}Every rule that matches runs, not just the first, in the order they
are listed.
@{bullet}A pattern that is not a valid expression is skipped with a warning
in the log; the other rules still run.
@{bullet}A match cannot be interrupted, and it runs on the server's event
loop: a pattern with nested repetition (@{tt}(a+)+@{utt}) can stall
everything for seconds. A rule that takes longer than 0.1 s is named in the
log.

@{h2}Placeholders

Parameter values may contain two kinds of placeholder:

@{table}
Placeholder | Filled from
{name} | the named group @{tt}(?P<name>...)@{utt} of the rule's expression -- something the user typed
{{ name }} | the session's template variable of that name -- the same value the prompt template gets
@{body}

@{tt}{{ name }}@{utt} is for state the user never types. The variables are
the agent's @{tt}template_vars@{utt}, overridden key by key by the session's
own (@{tt}/vars KEY=VALUE@{utt} in the chat, or a tool that sets them). A
sub-agent starts with the variables of the session that spawned it, so a
rule can name the parent's store without the task text mentioning it:

@{code yaml}
- match: "Task:\\s*(?P<task>\\w+)"
  tool: notes_manage_json
  params: {operation: read, doc: outline, namespace: "{{ project }}"}
@{body}

How they are filled:

@{bullet}Placeholders work at any depth -- inside nested mappings and lists
too.
@{bullet}A value that is exactly one placeholder becomes a number when the
text is a plain integer that reads back the same: @{tt}"42"@{utt} becomes
42, @{tt}"007"@{utt} stays @{tt}"007"@{utt}. A value with more around it
(@{tt}"chapter_{n}"@{utt}) stays text.
@{bullet}A filled-in value is not read again: a variable whose text
contains @{tt}{x}@{utt} is passed on as it is.
@{bullet}Values that are not text pass unchanged; what JSON cannot hold (an
unquoted YAML date) becomes text.
@{bullet}A placeholder with no such group or variable -- or a variable set
to null -- skips the whole rule, with a warning in the log. The tool is
never called with a literal @{tt}{doc}@{utt}.

@{h2}A rule is all or nothing

@{tt}max_calls_per_turn@{utt} and @{tt}dedup@{utt} work on whole rules:

@{bullet}A rule whose calls would go past the limit is skipped whole, with a
warning; the model makes those calls itself. A chain cut in half -- the
variable set, the content never loaded -- would be worse than no preload.
@{bullet}A rule is skipped as already done only when every one of its calls,
with the same parameters, is already somewhere in the conversation -- made
by the model or by an earlier preload. The comparison uses the filled-in
parameters; a rule the model already carried out differently runs again.

@{h2}Rules that change things

Nothing limits rules to reading. A rule may set a variable or write, and
the user's text chooses the parameters. Preloaded calls do not pass
@{tt}tool_approval@{utt} (see @{" The hook " link hook}). Write rules whose
match is specific enough that running them is always right.
@endnode

@node hook "The hook"
@{h1}The hook

@{tt}preload@{utt}, a @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} hook, off by default; its
time limit is 60 seconds.

@{h2}When it acts

Before every LLM call of an agent that has it on -- chat agents, sub-agents
and woken runs alike -- but it only acts on the first call of a turn: when
the last message is the user's. Notes a hook or the loop put behind it
(marked @{tt}injected_by@{utt}) are looked past. After a preload the last
message is a tool result, so the later calls of the turn leave it alone.

For a sub-agent the user message is its task. A woken run has no user
message -- its task is the run's own note -- so nothing is preloaded there.

@{h2}What it does

@{bullet}Finds the rules that match and fills in their parameters (see
@{" Writing rules " link rules}).
@{bullet}Runs the calls one after the other, each waiting for the one
before -- a later call may depend on what an earlier one set.
@{bullet}Appends, behind the user message, one pair per call: an assistant
message with the tool call, and the tool's answer (shown in
@{" What the model sees " link model}).

The calls go through the agent's own tool dispatch:

@{bullet}Only tools in the agent's @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} and not in
@{tt}tools.blocked@{utt}, the same patterns the model's tool list is built
from. An external MCP tool (a name with a dot) cannot be preloaded.
@{bullet}With the same session, request and user as the model's own calls:
a session-scoped tool answers from the right session, and the user is the
run's owner.
@{bullet}Without the @{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} and @{tt}post_tool_call@{utt}
hooks. @{tt}tool_approval@{utt} does not ask before a preloaded call, and
no hook that shortens or redacts tool results sees it.

@{h2}When something fails

@{table}
Case | What happens
the dispatch refuses the call (unknown tool, not allowed, blocked) | nothing is recorded for it, the rest of its rule's chain is dropped, the pairs before it stay, the next rule runs; a warning in the log
the tool raises | recorded as an error answer, the way the model's own call would be; the rest of its rule's chain is dropped, the next rule runs
the tool answers with an error | recorded like any answer; the chain goes on
a placeholder cannot be filled, a pattern is invalid | that rule is skipped, the others run
the time budget (max_seconds) is used up | no further call starts; each call left gets a "not run" answer
anything else goes wrong in the hook | logged; the turn runs without a preload
@{body}

A preload never stops the run. Whatever ran is in the conversation, so the
model does not call it again blind.

@{h2}Time

Two limits apply. @{tt}max_seconds@{utt} (45 s) is checked before each
call: once it is used up, the calls done so far are kept and every call
left is answered "not run" without running, so the model makes it itself.
A call that has started is never interrupted by it. Above that stands the
hook's own time limit of 60 seconds, enforced by the hook system: a single
call that runs past it ends the whole hook, and then nothing is appended,
not even for calls that finished. Keep @{tt}max_seconds@{utt} below it,
with room for the slowest call. The 60 seconds can be changed only for all
agents, under @{tt}hooks.overrides@{utt} in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}
(@{tt}tool_preload.preload: {timeout: 120}@{utt}); an agent's override
cannot.

@{h2}Order

It runs before the hooks of the categories @{tt}context_engineering@{utt},
@{tt}prompt_injection@{utt}, @{tt}message_validation@{utt} and
@{tt}message_debugging@{utt}: compaction sees an oversized preloaded result
in the same pass, and the hooks that read the end of the list see it with
the preload already there.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

For each call, two messages behind the user's, the same shape as the
model's own tool calls:

@{code json}
{"role": "assistant", "content": null,
 "tool_calls": [{"id": "preload_3f9c2a61b0", "type": "function",
                 "function": {"name": "notes_manage_json",
                              "arguments": "{\"operation\": \"read\", \"doc\": \"plan-a\"}"}}]}
{"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "preload_3f9c2a61b0", "name": "notes_manage_json",
 "content": "{\"status\": \"success\", \"data\": ...}"}
@{body}

To the model it looks as if it had made the call. Only the
@{tt}preload_@{utt} id and the assistant message's
@{tt}injected_by: tool_preload@{utt} mark tell them apart, and the mark is
never sent to a provider. The agent's system prompt is not told about
preloading.

A failing tool answers as the model's own call would:

@{code json}
{"error": "Tool 'notes_manage_json' execution failed: <message>", "type": "RuntimeError"}
@{body}

A call the time budget left unrun has an id starting with
@{tt}preload_skipped_@{utt} and answers:

@{code json}
{"error": "Not run: the preload time budget was used up. Call the tool yourself if you need it."}
@{body}

Such a call does not count as made: the rule runs again in a later turn.

There is no status line in the chat and no tool event while the preload
runs. The session's messages are the record, and every executed
preload is logged at INFO with its tool and parameters.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The pairs are appended behind the user message and never changed or moved
afterwards: every request stays a prefix of the next, so the provider's
prompt cache is untouched. They are kept with the session like the model's
own calls, and a later turn's preload lands behind that turn's user
message. Other plugins see the marked assistant message as not written by
the model: compaction counts the preloaded results as part of the round no
request has carried yet.

The plugin sets no size limit on a result: what the tool returns goes in
whole, as it would for the model's own call. For an agent that runs
@{tt}context_engineer@{utt}, a result larger than a quarter of the model's
context window (its default) is stored away and replaced by a pointer in
the same pass, before it is sent; otherwise keep preloads to tools whose answers are
small, and let the model page through the rest.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Preloaded calls bypass @{tt}tool_approval@{utt} and every other
tool-call hook.
@{bullet}One call that outlasts the hook's time limit ends the hook; then
no call of that turn is recorded, though they ran.
@{bullet}No size limit of its own on a result.
@{bullet}A match cannot be interrupted; a slow pattern stalls the server.
@endnode
